.docname {Socket}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | WebSocket | A connection the browser keeps open, so the server can push | One per open tab |
    | Room | A group of connections that get the same messages | `building:{id}`, `domain:{name}` |
    | Subscribe | A browser asks to join one building's room | Opening the library page |
    | Claims header | Who the caller is, set by the edge | `x-gateway-claims` |
    | Functional core | Plain functions with no network, easy to test | "which room is this message for?" |
    | Imperative shell | The part that does the network work around the core | Redis, WebSocket |

Realises part of the [Telemetry Distribution](../domain-model/domain-distribution.qd) domain, with `dashboard`: it carries live data to the people following a building. Rust, no database. Code-level page: [Socket Service](../services/socket.qd).

---

## Who talks to it

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        D["dashboard"] -. "one feed per building" .-> S["socket"]
        N["notification"] -. "alerts" .-> S
        Tw["digital-twin"] -. "device dots" .-> S
        S -- "who owns this building?" --> Tw
        S <--> C["client, over WebSocket"]

Dotted: Redis.

| Comes in on | Goes to room | Browser event |
|---|---|---|
| A building's feed | `building:{id}` | `telemetry` |
| `notifications` | `domain:{name}` | `notification` |
| A building's device dots | `building:{id}` | Device positions |

Messages pass through unread, apart from finding the room.

---

## Inside

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        subgraph Shell["Shell: network work"]
            CON["Connect and subscribe"]
            LOOP["Redis listener"]
            LOOK["Ownership lookup, cached"]
            SWEEP["Lifetime sweep"]
        end
        subgraph Core["Core: plain functions"]
            AUTH["Who is this? May they read?"]
            ROUTE["Which room is this message for?"]
            LIFE["How long may this connection live?"]
        end
        CON --> AUTH
        CON --> LOOK
        LOOP --> ROUTE
        SWEEP --> LIFE

- The core does no network work, so it is tested with plain inputs.
- A test fails the build if the core imports a network library, or names the shell.
- Room names and the header name are written in one place each, so they cannot drift.

---

## Connecting and subscribing

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant C as client
        participant S as socket
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        C->>S: connect, with the cookie
        Note over S: read claims, join domain rooms
        C->>S: subscribe to building 42
        S->>Tw: who owns building 42?
        Tw-->>S: northfield-university
        Note over S: shared organization? join building:42
        S-->>C: live data from now on

- **Joining the room is the permission check.** Every message to that room then goes out with no further check.
- Organization rooms come from the claims at connect. Building rooms need a subscribe each.

| Ownership answer | Kept for 60 s? | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Owners found | Yes | Join if an organization is shared |
| No owners, or unknown building | Yes | Refused |
| Lookup failed | No | Refused, the next try asks again |

- Caching "unknown" stops a client from guessing ids to flood `digital-twin`.
- Not caching failures means a short `digital-twin` hiccup does not blank screens for a minute.
- Refusals are counted apart: a stranger knocking and `digital-twin` down must not look the same.

---

## How long a connection lives

- At most about 15 minutes, like the pass. Then `socket` closes it.
- Each connection gets a little less, at random, up to an eighth. Otherwise every client from one deploy would reconnect in the same second, forever.
- `client` reconnects after a short wait, with a fresh cookie. That reconnect is the new identity check.
- After a reconnect, `client` subscribes to its buildings again and fetches the gap.

**Example.** The permission check runs only at subscribe. Ada leaves Northfield at 10:00. Her open tab keeps getting library data until its connection is closed, at most about 15 minutes later.

---

## Choices

| Choice | Why |
|---|---|
| No domain model, no database | It only carries messages; it can be replaced at any time |
| Trust the edge's claims | The edge already checked the pass; no second check |
| Building data per building room, alerts per organization room | No organization sees another's data |
| A message with no organization is skipped | Sending it to everyone would leak names across customers |

---

## Gaps

- **One copy only.** More copies would need sticky sessions and a shared adapter so every copy hears every room.
- **A connection can outlive its pass** by up to its lifetime. The pass carries no end time a connection could check.
